Step 1.6: Blocked Call Audit Trail + Overlay Trigger Fix
Two confirmed bugs in one step. Every BLOCK decision leaves zero trace anywhere. And the overlay trigger reads the wrong field.

Bug 1 — Zero audit trail:
Every BLOCK decision must write to two destinations simultaneously:
CallLogEntry — permanent audit record: decision = 'BLOCK', spamCategory, confidence, matchedSources.
PendingCardEntity — short-lived digest queue entry: phoneNumber, timestamp, decision, confidence, decisionSource, dismissed = false.
Keep setSkipCallLog(true) and setSkipNotification(true) at the Android level — correct behavior preserved.
Bug 2 — Overlay trigger uses wrong field (confirmed in live code):
// CURRENT (wrong):
if (callInfo.spamStatus == "LIKELY SPAM" || callInfo.spamStatus == "SPAM") {
// CORRECT:
if (callInfo.callDecision == CallDecision.BLOCK) {
spamStatus is a classification label. callDecision is the actual action taken. All overlay, card-creation, and notification logic must key off callDecision, not spamStatus.
Deliverable: CallScreeningService writing both records on BLOCK, with overlay trigger corrected to use CallDecision enum.

Step 1.7: Post-Call Digest Card System
Post-call blocked-call notification is not a live overlay during an active call. It is a batched digest surfaced after calls end, cadence controlled by the aggressiveness setting in SettingEntry.
Near-real-time tier: card appears shortly after call ends.
Batched tiers: unseen PendingCardEntity records accumulate and surface every 2, 4, or 8 hours via WorkManager periodic worker.
Silent tier: events log to CallLogEntry only. PendingCardEntity is still created but the worker never surfaces it.
User: swipe individual cards to dismiss (deletes PendingCardEntity row). Tap 'Dismiss All' (deletes all dismissed=false rows).
Each card: number, timestamp, reason, confidence, one-tap 'not spam' → unblock + whitelist via BlocklistRepository.
Implementation: CallOverlayService / CallOverlayViewModel render the card stack. PostCallNotifier retired or demoted to fallback for devices where overlay permission is denied.
PendingCardEntity must be added to SignalGateDatabase @Database annotation and a new PendingCardDao created before the card system is built.
Step 1.8: Permission Health Check Screen
Four distinct Android grant mechanisms, all surfaced in one screen with guided recovery, re-evaluating live state every time it opens.
Standard runtime permissions (READ_CONTACTS, POST_NOTIFICATIONS, READ_CALL_LOG): shared composable extracted from PermissionSettingsScreen.kt — reusable in both wizard and this screen.
Overlay special permission: Settings.ACTION_MANAGE_OVERLAY_PERMISSION intent. Button exists in SettingsScreens.kt — consolidate here.
Call screening role: RoleManager intent (Step 1.5). Currently completely absent.
Battery optimization exemption: ACTION_REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS. Per-OEM (Samsung/Xiaomi/Huawei/OnePlus) deep links with graceful fallback where APIs don't exist.
Lost call-screening role: persistent banner on consumer dashboard, not just this screen.
This screen is the primary support tool for the non-technical user getting confusing behavior. It is the difference between an uninstall and a fix.

Step 1.9: Gray-Zone Heuristics for Unknown Callers
Calls that pass all blocklist filters but are not in contacts are unconditionally allowed. Heuristics slot in as step 4.5 in CallScreeningEngine's existing 5-step priority chain.
Light tier (cheap, on-device): toll-free/premium prefix detection, repeated/sequential digit patterns, area-code vs SIM-region mismatch, call-velocity bursts from same prefix block.
Mid tier: surface partial confidence BloomFilterEngine/PrecedenceEngine already compute but discard below the block threshold. Near-miss becomes a real signal.
Tie to SignalGateMode: LPP skips heuristics, MULTI_GATE applies light tier, FULL_THROTTLE applies light + mid.
Default disposition for gray-zone: advisory not blocking. Label 'Caution', surface in post-call digest for one-tap review. False positives on real callers cost more trust than missed spam.
Step 1.10: Quick Temporary Block — Non-Contacts for N Hours
Single toggle on consumer dashboard: block all non-contact calls for 2, 4, or 8 hours.
Store expiry timestamp in SettingEntry (key: block_non_contacts_until). Check System.currentTimeMillis() > expiry inline at screening time. No WorkManager needed.
'Contacts' means the curated allowlist from ContactsViewModel, not the full device contact list.
Toggle auto-cancels at expiry with no user action.
