Step 1.12: SQLCipher Key Management — Keystore Design
CRITICAL: SecureDatabase.kt passphrase is hardcoded as 'your-secure-passphrase'. Pre-launch blocker. The comment says 'Derive from Biometric/KeyStore in prod' — the biometric approach has a fundamental flaw for background service context.

Two separate concerns that must not be conflated:
Database key — needed by CallScreeningService at any time, including when phone is locked. No biometric prompt possible. Correct approach:
At first install: generate a cryptographically random 256-bit key.
Store in Android Keystore with setUserAuthenticationRequired(false) and setUnlockedDeviceRequired(false).
On every DB open: retrieve silently from Keystore, pass to SupportOpenHelperFactory.
User sees nothing. Key is hardware-backed. Background service always has access.
Optional biometric UI gate — separate feature gating the history screen in the UI. NOT the source of the encryption key.
V1 decision: skip the biometric history gate. Add as opt-in Settings toggle in a future release.
Rationale: adds friction on a screen showing spam numbers, not financial data. Wrong trade-off for v1.
Deliverable: Keystore-Key-Design.md written before any code changes to SecureDatabase.kt. Covers key generation, storage, retrieval, rotation on reinstall, and recovery strategy.

