Phase 3: Security Hardening
Goal: Fortify security boundaries and validate critical runtime paths.
Step 3.1: Input Validation — Presentation Layer
All user inputs validated before reaching ViewModels.
SanitizationEngine (Layer 2) for phone number format and URL checking.
Rejection of malformed inputs at the UI perimeter — nothing unvalidated reaches domain logic.
Step 3.2: CallScreeningEngine Policy Validation
Verify threat-source precedence rules enforce correctly across all source types.
Test edge cases: overlapping block/allow rules, wildcard patterns, null inputs, gray-zone confidence thresholds.
CallScreeningService delegates to engine without any inline decision logic in the service class itself.
PhoneStateReceiver triggers screening without race conditions.
Step 3.3: Transport Layer Data Governance
CallLogRepository, DataSourceRepository, SyncHistoryRepository, BlocklistRepository are the sole data access points.
No Layer 5 or 6 component accesses Room directly.
All repository methods enforce transaction boundaries with rollback on failure.
Step 3.4: Network Communication Security
OkHttp configured with TLS 1.3 enforcement and certificate pinning.
Network operations isolated from UI thread via coroutines with structured concurrency.
CRITICAL: TrustedSourceCatalog.kt FTC/FCC data source URLs are placeholders. Verify and replace before shipping.

Step 3.5: Dependency Scanning — CI Integration
OWASP Dependency-Check integrated into CI pipeline — runs on every push.
Blocks merge on any critical/high vulnerability in a direct dependency.
Monthly manual review of transitive dependency advisories.
Step 3.6: Mobile Security Analysis
MobSF (Mobile Security Framework) static + dynamic analysis against the release APK before each major release.
MobSF covers: data leakage paths, exported component exposure, permission misuse, hardcoded strings. Correct tooling for Android. Classical web-DAST does not apply to this app.
Confirm LogcatViewerScreen is absent from release APK via MobSF component scan.
Confirm hardcoded SQLCipher passphrase absent — Keystore derivation confirmed.
Step 3.7: Performance Targets
Post-call digest card render: within 200ms of CallScreeningService decision.
Cold startup time: under 1.5 seconds.
Consumer dashboard stat counters: update within 100ms of event.


Phase 4: Repository Governance and Workflow Hardening
Goal: Establish tooling and conventions that keep the codebase clean, auditable, and corruption-free.
Step 4.1: Pre-Delivery Verification Protocol
Before any file is delivered for commit, the following checks must be run programmatically:
Brace balance: count opening vs closing braces in the file — must match exactly.
No unintended shell expansion patterns: confirm no unescaped heredoc-style patterns that would expand in a shell context.
Import completeness: every import statement in a Kotlin file must reference a class that actually exists in the repo or declared dependencies. No forward references to unbuilt files.
These checks apply to every .kt, .gradle, .yml, and .json file before it is written to the branch.
Step 4.2: Pre-Push Integrity Hook
Tracked .githooks/pre-push script blocking pushes when corruption is detected. Activate locally with:
git config core.hooksPath .githooks
git diff --check: blocks on trailing whitespace and conflict markers.
yamllint .github/workflows/*.yml: blocks on YAML syntax errors.
Install yamllint:
pip install --user yamllint --break-system-packages
Hook committed to .githooks/ — travels with every clone. core.hooksPath must be set locally on each device.
Status: hook script exists but not yet confirmed active in Termux. Open task until explicitly verified.

